<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John VII, Count of Nassau-Siegen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as John the Middle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John VII, Count of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, known as John the Middle, received Siegen after the death of his father, John VI of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in 1606. A leading Calvinist military reformer, he wrote on the drill methods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army and founded a military academy at Siegen in 1616. His lands were divided among his sons after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vii-count-of-nassau-siegen.jpg" alt="John VII, Count of Nassau-Siegen" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Jan van Ravesteyn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_de_middelste_van_Nassau-Siegen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born at Siegen Castle on 7 June 1561, the second son of Count John VI the Elder and his first wife, Landgravine Elisabeth of Leuchtenberg. After the Counts' School in Siegen he went in 1576 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">Heidelberg University</a> with his brothers William Louis, George and Philip and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a>, but left the next year at his father's wish. On 9 December 1581 he married Countess Magdalene of Waldeck-Wildungen, a match of his own choosing that his father accepted. He was first called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-viii-count-of-nassau-siegen">John the Younger</a> and became John the Middle once his own son John came of age; historians numbered the counts only in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He saw his first campaign in 1583 under Count Palatine John Casimir and from 1584 strengthened the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a> and Dillenburg Castle. When his father introduced general conscription, John made sure the men were trained: he ordered standard rifles of one calibre, so that one soldier could give another bullets, dressed them in a uniform, then something new, and invented the foot drill. His Kriegsbuch gathered the military knowledge of the time with many new ideas and contributed to the reform of the Dutch States Army by his cousin Maurice. He served in that army, was colonel general of the Palatinate and commander-in-chief of the Swedish army.</p><p>He worked for Protestant unity against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, in 1598 even promoting the election of Maurice as German king. After Duke John William of Jülich-Cleves-Berg died childless on 25 March 1609, John brought Count Palatine Wolfgang William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> and Elector John Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> to agree on a common course at Dortmund on 10 June 1609.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His father died on 8 October 1606, and on 30 March 1607 the brothers divided their lands: John received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, Freudenberg, Netphen, Hilchenbach, Ferndorf and the Haingericht, and their treaty forbade their heirs to leave the Reformed confession. He died at Siegen Castle on 27 September 1623, the father of 25 children by two marriages, and was buried in the St. Nicholas Church in Siegen.</p><p>A dispute followed at the reading of his will on 11 December 1623. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-viii-count-of-nassau-siegen">John the Younger</a>, holding an imperial decree, received the homage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> on 12 January 1624 after slipping horsemen into the town in a snowstorm, but he then ceded the Hilchenbach district to his brother William, and until 1645 the county had two governments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John VII of Nassau-Siegen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the death of Count John VI of Nassau-Dillenburg in 1606, his lands were divided and his son <b>John VII</b> received Siegen.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-nassau-siegen">Principality of Nassau-Siegen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oberes Schloss, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Oberes Schloss, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.03&amp;year=1601" aria-label="Oberes Schloss on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John VII, Count of Nassau-Siegen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5432 5751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5432 5751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.03&amp;year=1601">What was here in 1601? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
